  "worked_solution": "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one by one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{′}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1})}^{′}=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is not differentiable at this points.\n\n- The derivative of  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is\n\n$$\n{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ except the points $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$",
